\paragraph{Horizontal drift and isotropic regularization}
\label{sec:riccati-per-axis-limits}
The most striking horizontal diagnostic is not a tuned axis ratio but a large
separation in the disturbance itself.  After attitude compensation, a small
tilt error leaks gravity into horizontal acceleration approximately as
\begin{equation}
\boxed{
\vct\varepsilon_{a,H}
\simeq
\begin{bmatrix}
g\,\delta\theta_y\\-g\,\delta\theta_x
\end{bmatrix}
+\vct b_{a,H}+\vct n_H .}
\label{eq:riccati-axis-tilt-leak}
\end{equation}
The vertical channel has no corresponding first-order $g\,\delta\theta$ term,
and without GNSS or another horizontal kinematic reference the residual
horizontal bias is also less observable.  Direct drift-band diagnostics over
the eight stationary calibration records put the world-$x$ and world-$y$
low-frequency acceleration-error intensities at approximately $65$ and $102$
times the vertical value, respectively.

Yet the full-filter experiments do not support the intuitive response
``regularize the noisy axis much harder.''  The reduced theorem of
Sec.~\ref{sec:riccati-mis-specified-pseudo} explains why that lever is weak:
$q_{\mathrm{eff}}$ enters the reduced preferred regularizer through only the
$1/14$ power, so even a factor-of-ten error in drift intensity changes
$r_{S,\mathrm{red}}^{\star}$ by only
$10^{1/14}\simeq1.18$.  At the same reduced optimum, residual error carries the
much stronger $q_{\mathrm{eff}}^{4/7}$ dependence.  A large horizontal
disturbance can therefore dominate the residual motion error while moving the
useful regularization scale only modestly.

The desired-motion term pulls in the opposite direction.  The horizontal
$m_{-4}$ content is a projection of real directional orbital motion, so its
world-$x/world-y$ split changes with wave heading.  The present simulations
sample too few independent headings to identify universal separate $x$ and $y$
regularization constants from those moments.  The defensible full-estimator
finding is instead that the current \emph{isotropic} $r_S$ performs well over
the tested directional cases.  This shifts the engineering priority from
``regularize the noisy horizontal axis harder'' toward reducing the actual
low-frequency acceleration error---better tilt, better accelerometer-bias
observability, GNSS, or another horizontal reference.  The $65$--$102\times$
ratios are simulation diagnostics, not universal ocean constants, and the
conclusion must be re-tested on a broader multidirectional ensemble.

\subsection{Attitude and adaptation behavior}

