\subsection{Hard-iron correction result}
\label{sec:mag-hi-results}

The hard-iron correction of Sec.~\ref{sec:mag-hard-iron} is evaluated under the
deterministic protocol rather than the ten-seed OU-family comparison: one
realization per stationary record, default sensor-error draw, and the final
\SI{900}{s}.  With the correction enabled, yaw RMS improves on every one of the
eight records.  The eight-record mean falls from
\SI{1.835}{\degree} to \SI{0.822}{\degree}, and the worst record falls from
\SI{2.162}{\degree} to \SI{1.063}{\degree}.  Pitch improves slightly, while
roll and displacement change negligibly.  The matched OU--II baseline carries
the same magnetic correction, so this improvement does not contribute to the
reported OU--III-minus-OU--II translational difference.

\begin{table}[t]
  \centering
  \caption{Yaw RMS with the continuous hard-iron correction disabled and
           enabled (deterministic protocol, final \SI{900}{s}).}
  \label{tab:mag-hi-yaw}
  \setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
  \renewcommand{\arraystretch}{1.08}
  \footnotesize
  \sisetup{detect-all}
  \begin{tabular}{@{}l
                  S[table-format=1.2]
                  S[table-format=1.3]
                  S[table-format=1.3]
                  S[table-format=1.3]
                  S[table-format=1.3]
                  @{}}
    \toprule
    {Case} & {$H_s$} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Yaw RMS (\si{\degree})}
           & \multicolumn{2}{c}{Pitch RMS (\si{\degree})} \\
